## Frontend Architecture (React + TypeScript)

### Module-Based Structure

```
modules/
├── auth/
│   ├── pages/              # Page components
│   ├── components/         # Domain components
│   ├── hooks/              # Custom hooks
│   ├── services/           # API clients
│   └── types/              # Interfaces
├── sales/
│   ├── pages/
│   ├── components/
│   ├── hooks/
│   ├── services/
│   └── types/
...
```

**Rule:** Each module is self-contained. Share via hooks, not direct imports.

### Component Organization

**Files:** `frontend/src/modules/*/components/`

**Patterns:**
- **Functional components only** (no class components)
- **Fully typed props** (no `any`)
- **Custom hooks** for logic extraction
- **Error boundaries** for risky components

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT: Fully typed, error-handled
interface SalesOrderTableProps {
  orders: SalesOrder[];
  onSelect: (id: string) => void;
  loading?: boolean;
}

export const SalesOrderTable: React.FC<SalesOrderTableProps> = ({
  orders,
  onSelect,
  loading = false
}) => {
  if (loading) return <LoadingSpinner />;
  if (!orders.length) return <EmptyState />;
  
  return (
    <table>
      {orders.map(o => (
        <tr key={o.id} onClick={() => onSelect(o.id)}>
          <td>{o.number}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </table>
  );
};
```

### State Management

**Pattern:** useState for local, useReducer for complex, Context for global

```typescript
// ✅ Custom hook for reusable logic
export const useSalesOrders = () => {
  const [orders, setOrders] = React.useState<SalesOrder[]>([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = React.useState(true);
  
  React.useEffect(() => {
    const fetch = async () => {
      try {
        const data = await api.orders.list();
        setOrders(data);
      } finally {
        setLoading(false);
      }
    };
    fetch();
  }, []);
  
  return { orders, loading };
};
```

